pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Trace a while or for loop pass by pass, including break, continue and the loop else"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can trace a while or for loop pass by pass, including break, continue and the loop else, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter3.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Choose between while and for, and between range, enumerate and zip, for a given repetition"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can choose between while and for, and between range, enumerate and zip, for a given repetition, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter3.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Predict the values range() makes from one, two and three arguments, and explain why a range is not a list"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can predict the values range() makes from one, two and three arguments, and explain why a range is not a list, at the understand level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter3.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand  .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Use the import forms and sys.exit() correctly, and justify the import style PEP 8 recommends"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can use the import forms and sys.exit() correctly, and justify the import style PEP 8 recommends, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter3.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does list(range(5, -1, -1)) produce?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The stop value -1 is excluded, but 0 comes before it when counting down, so 0 is included." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A negative step counts down, so the range is not empty when the start is above the stop." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0]" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[]" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "When does the else clause of a for or while loop run?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A loop can have an else clause." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "It runs after a full loop too; running zero times is just one way of finishing without a break." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Never; else belongs only to if" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "When the loop finishes without executing break" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Only when the loop ran zero times" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What is the value of i after for i in range(3): pass?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Names in the target list are not deleted when the loop ends." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "range(3) stops before 3, so the last value assigned is 2." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It no longer exists" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "3" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "2" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does list(zip('abc', [1, 2], strict=True)) do?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is what zip does by default, stopping at the shortest; strict=True changes it." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "zip never pads with None; itertools.zip_longest does." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Raises ValueError, because the lengths differ" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Returns [('a', 1), ('b', 2)]" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Returns [('a', 1), ('b', 2), ('c', None)]" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Which import style does PEP 8 advise against?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is the style PEP 8 recommends: imports on separate lines." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A plain import is fine; it keeps every name under its module's name." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "import os on one line and import sys on the next" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "from random import *" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "import random" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "You need each item of a list together with its position. Which loop is the best fit?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "It works, but enumerate gives position and item directly, as the tutorial advises." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "It needs a counter you must remember to update, an easy way to write an endless loop." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "for i in range(len(a)):" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "while i < len(a):" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "for i, x in enumerate(a):" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A program calls sys.exit() inside a try block that has a finally clause. What happens?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "sys.exit() raises SystemExit, and finally clauses are honored as the exception passes." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "sys.exit() only raises an exception; it can be called anywhere." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The program exits at once and the finally clause is skipped" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The finally clause still runs, and then the program exits" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "sys.exit() only works outside try blocks" .
pg:Repetition a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Repetition"@en ;
    skos:definition "Repetition is the ability of a program to run the same block of code more than once, and it is the subject of the first half of chapter 3. Chapter 2 showed how a program chooses between blocks, so that some lines run and others are skipped; this chapter adds the loop, a statement that sends execution back to the start of a block so that the same lines can serve many values (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_Repetition> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Repetition" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Repetition .
pg:LoopBasics a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Loop basics"@en ;
    skos:definition "The loop basics are the two ideas on which every loop statement rests: the loop itself, which is a block that Python runs again and again, and the iteration, which is one run of that block. The two words are used constantly in the book and in Python's documentation, so they are defined before the particular loop statements are introduced."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_LoopBasics> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoopBasics" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Repetition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoopBasics .
pg:Loop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "A loop is a statement that makes Python execute a block of code over and over. Python has two kinds, the while loop and the for loop, and each is written as a line that ends with a colon followed by an indented block, which the book calls the loop's clause (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_Loop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Loop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Loop .
pg:Iteration a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Iteration"@en ;
    skos:definition "An iteration is a single execution of a loop's block. A loop that runs its block five times is said to make five iterations, and the book uses the word in exactly this sense when it explains that the condition of a while loop is checked at the start of each iteration, that is, each time the loop is executed (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_Iteration> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Iteration" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Iteration .
pg:ConditionLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Condition loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "A condition loop is a loop whose length is decided by a test instead of by a count. The while statement is the loop of this kind, and the infinite loop is the special case in which the test can never fail (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ConditionLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ConditionLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Repetition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ConditionLoop .
pg:WhileStatement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "While statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "A while statement repeats its block for as long as its condition is true. In code it always consists of the while keyword, a condition, a colon and, starting on the next line, an indented block, which the book calls the while clause or while block (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_WhileStatement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhileStatement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConditionLoop .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhileStatement .
pg:InfiniteLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Infinite loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "An infinite loop is a loop whose condition is always true, and the plainest form is while True. The expression True always evaluates to the value True, so the test at the top can never end the loop (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_InfiniteLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InfiniteLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConditionLoop .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InfiniteLoop .
pg:CountedLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Counted loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "A counted loop is a loop whose number of iterations is fixed before it starts, because it walks through a sequence of values that already exists. The for statement is the loop of this kind, and the two sections that follow it show how it relates to the while loop and how a loop gathers a result (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_CountedLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CountedLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Repetition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CountedLoop .
pg:ForStatement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "For statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "A for statement runs its block once for each item of an iterable, assigning the item to its loop variable before each run. The book describes the statement as the for keyword, a variable name, the in keyword, a call to range() with up to three integers, a colon and an indented block (Sweigart, 2025), and the language reference generalises it: the statement iterates over the elements of a sequence or of any other iterable object (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ForStatement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ForStatement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CountedLoop .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ForStatement .
pg:ForWhileEquivalence a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "For while equivalence"@en ;
    skos:definition "The equivalent while loop is the rewriting of a for loop over a range as a while loop with an explicit counter. The counter is given its first value before the loop, the condition compares it with the limit, and the last line of the block increases it."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ForWhileEquivalence> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ForWhileEquivalence" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CountedLoop .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ForWhileEquivalence .
pg:Accumulator a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Accumulator"@en ;
    skos:definition "An accumulator is a variable that collects a result across the iterations of a loop. It is given a known starting value before the loop, such as 0 for a sum, and every iteration updates it with the current item, as in total = total + num."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_Accumulator> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Accumulator" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CountedLoop .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Accumulator .
pg:LoopComposition a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Loops in programs"@en ;
    skos:definition "Loops in programs are the ways in which loops are combined with the other statements of the chapter into working code: a loop placed inside another loop, and the two complete programs of the chapter, Guess the Number and Rock, Paper, Scissors (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_LoopComposition> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoopComposition" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Repetition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoopComposition .
pg:NestedLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Nested loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "A nested loop is a loop that is placed inside the block of another loop. The outer loop starts an iteration, the inner loop then runs from its beginning to its end, and only afterwards does the outer loop continue with its next iteration."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_NestedLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NestedLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopComposition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NestedLoop .
pg:GuessTheNumber a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Guess the number"@en ;
    skos:definition "Guess the Number is the first complete program of the chapter, a game in which the computer picks a secret number between 1 and 20 and the player has six chances to find it, receiving the hint too low or too high after each wrong guess (Sweigart, 2025). It combines an import statement, a call to random.randint(), a for loop over range(1, 7), conversions with int() and str(), an if, elif and else chain, and a break."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_GuessTheNumber> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GuessTheNumber" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopComposition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GuessTheNumber .
pg:RockPaperScissors a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rock paper scissors"@en ;
    skos:definition "Rock, Paper, Scissors is the second complete program of the chapter. The player types r, p or s, the computer chooses a move at random, the program announces who has won, and a running score of wins, losses and ties is printed before every game; typing q ends the program (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_RockPaperScissors> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RockPaperScissors" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopComposition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RockPaperScissors .
pg:Sequence a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sequence"@en ;
    skos:definition "In chapter 3 the word sequence stands for whatever a for loop can walk through. Python's glossary gives the strict meaning: a sequence is an iterable that supports efficient access to its elements through integer indices and that has a length, and the built-in sequence types include list, str, tuple and bytes (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_Sequence> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Sequence" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Sequence .
pg:RangeSequence a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Range sequence"@en ;
    skos:definition "A range sequence is the arithmetic progression of integers that the built-in range produces. The Python tutorial describes the function as one that generates arithmetic progressions, and the reference documentation adds that range is really an immutable sequence type, one whose contents cannot be changed after it is created, and not a function (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_RangeSequence> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RangeSequence" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Sequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RangeSequence .
pg:RangeStop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Range stop"@en ;
    skos:definition "Called with a single argument, range(stop) stands for the whole numbers 0, 1, 2 and so on up to the number just before stop. The tutorial puts it this way: the given end point is never part of the generated sequence, and range(10) generates 10 values, which are the legal indices for the items of a sequence of length 10 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_RangeStop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RangeStop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeSequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RangeStop .
pg:RangeStartStop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Range start stop"@en ;
    skos:definition "With two arguments, range(start, stop) begins at start and ends just before stop. The book introduces it as the way to follow any sequence of integers, including one that starts at a number other than zero, and shows that for i in range(12, 16) prints 12, 13, 14 and 15 (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_RangeStartStop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RangeStartStop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeSequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RangeStartStop .
pg:RangeStep a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Range step"@en ;
    skos:definition "A third argument to range is the step, the amount by which the value is increased after each iteration. The call range(0, 10, 2) therefore counts from 0 to 8 in intervals of two, and the book shows that its loop prints 0, 2, 4, 6 and 8 (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_RangeStep> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RangeStep" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeSequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RangeStep .
pg:RangeDescending a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Range descending"@en ;
    skos:definition "A negative step makes a range count down instead of up. The book's example for i in range(5, -1, -1) prints the numbers from five down to zero, and it describes the negative step as the way to make the for loop count down (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_RangeDescending> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RangeDescending" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeSequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RangeDescending .
pg:LazyRange a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Lazy range"@en ;
    skos:definition "A range is lazy: it does not store its values but produces them when they are asked for. The tutorial shows that printing range(10) displays range(0, 10) and explains that the object behaves in many ways as if it were a list, but in fact it isn't; it returns the successive items of the desired sequence when you iterate over it, without really making the list, thus saving space (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_LazyRange> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LazyRange" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeSequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LazyRange .
pg:HalfOpenRange a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Half-open range"@en ;
    skos:definition "Python writes ranges in the closed, open format: the start is included and the end is excluded. The book explains in a separate box why the loop goes up to but not including the number given, and says that in programming ranges are often specified in this way because it leads to fewer bugs (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_HalfOpenRange> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HalfOpenRange" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeSequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HalfOpenRange .
pg:RangeOperations a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Range operations"@en ;
    skos:definition "A range is a full sequence and supports most of the operations that lists and strings have. The library documentation lists length, membership tests with in, indexing with positive and negative positions, slicing and index lookup, and says that ranges implement all of the common sequence operations except concatenation and repetition (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_RangeOperations> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RangeOperations" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeSequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RangeOperations .
pg:IterationHelper a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Iteration helper"@en ;
    skos:definition "Iteration helpers are built-in functions that wrap an iterable so that a loop sees more than its plain items. The group of the chapter has two of them: enumerate adds a running count to each item, and zip walks through several iterables side by side."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_IterationHelper> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IterationHelper" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Sequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IterationHelper .
pg:EnumerateFunction a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Enumerate function"@en ;
    skos:definition "The enumerate function takes an iterable and returns an iterator of pairs, each made of a count and the corresponding item. The library documentation shows list(enumerate(seasons)) giving [(0, 'Spring'), (1, 'Summer'), (2, 'Fall'), (3, 'Winter')], and says that the count begins at start, which defaults to 0 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_EnumerateFunction> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EnumerateFunction" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:IterationHelper .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EnumerateFunction .
pg:ZipStrict a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Zip strict"@en ;
    skos:definition "The zip function walks through several iterables in parallel and produces tuples, the first holding the first item of each iterable, the second the second items, and so on (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Since Python 3.10 it takes an optional keyword argument strict, an argument that is written with its name; with strict=True the iterables must have the same length."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ZipStrict> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ZipStrict" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:IterationHelper .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ZipStrict .
pg:TargetUnpacking a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Unpacking in a loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "Unpacking is the assignment of the parts of a sequence to several variables at once. In a for statement the variable after the keyword for can be a list of names, and each item of the iterable is assigned to that list by the ordinary rules of assignment (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_TargetUnpacking> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TargetUnpacking" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:IterationHelper .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TargetUnpacking .
pg:IterationProtocol a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Iteration protocol"@en ;
    skos:definition "The iteration protocol is the agreement between a for loop and the objects it walks through. An object that can be walked through is an iterable; the loop asks it for an iterator; and the iterator hands out the items one at a time and announces when there are no more."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_IterationProtocol> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IterationProtocol" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Sequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IterationProtocol .
pg:IterableObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Iterable"@en ;
    skos:definition "An iterable is an object capable of returning its members one at a time. Python's glossary lists the sequence types, such as list, str and tuple, and some other types such as dict and file objects as examples, and adds that iterables can be used in a for loop and in many other places where a sequence is needed, such as zip() and map() (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_IterableObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IterableObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:IterationProtocol .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IterableObject .
pg:IteratorObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Iterator"@en ;
    skos:definition "An iterator is an object representing a stream of data. According to the glossary, repeated calls to its __next__() method, or passing it to the built-in function next(), return successive items of the stream, and when no more data are available a StopIteration exception is raised instead (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_IteratorObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IteratorObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:IterationProtocol .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IteratorObject .
pg:CollectionValue a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Collection value"@en ;
    skos:definition "A collection value is a value that holds other values. The chapter touches four of them: the sequence in general, the list, the tuple and the dictionary."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_CollectionValue> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CollectionValue" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Sequence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CollectionValue .
pg:SequenceKind a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sequence type"@en ;
    skos:definition "A sequence is an ordered collection whose members have positions. The glossary defines it as an iterable that supports efficient element access using integer indices and that has a length, and names list, str, tuple and bytes among the built-in sequence types (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_SequenceKind> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SequenceKind" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CollectionValue .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SequenceKind .
pg:ListValue a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "List"@en ;
    skos:definition "A list is an ordered collection written with its items between square brackets and separated by commas. The reference documentation describes the type as a mutable sequence (Python Software Foundation, 2026), and the glossary adds that, despite its name, a list is more akin to an array in other languages than to a linked list, since access to its elements is fast (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ListValue> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ListValue" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CollectionValue .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ListValue .
pg:TupleValue a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tuple"@en ;
    skos:definition "A tuple is a sequence of values separated by commas, and on output it is always shown in parentheses. The tutorial describes tuples as immutable and usually containing a heterogeneous sequence of elements that are accessed via unpacking or indexing (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_TupleValue> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TupleValue" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CollectionValue .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TupleValue .
pg:DictionaryValue a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Dictionary"@en ;
    skos:definition "A dictionary is a collection of key and value pairs, written between braces. The glossary describes it as an associative array in which arbitrary keys are mapped to values, and the tutorial adds that, unlike sequences, which are indexed by a range of numbers, dictionaries are indexed by keys (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_DictionaryValue> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DictionaryValue" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CollectionValue .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DictionaryValue .
pg:LoopControl a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Loop control"@en ;
    skos:definition "Loop control is the set of statements and calls that change the course of a loop from the inside. A loop normally runs until its condition fails or its sequence is used up, but the program can also leave it early, skip a single iteration, or react to what happened when the loop ended."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_LoopControl> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoopControl" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoopControl .
pg:EarlyExit a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Early exit"@en ;
    skos:definition "An early exit leaves a loop before its condition fails or its sequence is exhausted. The chapter presents the break statement, the sys.exit() call and, as the way to stop a runaway program, the interrupt from the keyboard; the return statement, which leaves the function that holds the loop, completes the list (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_EarlyExit> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EarlyExit" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopControl .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EarlyExit .
pg:BreakStatement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Break statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "A break statement ends the loop that contains it. In code it consists of the single keyword break, and when execution reaches it the program leaves the loop immediately (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_BreakStatement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BreakStatement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EarlyExit .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BreakStatement .
pg:ExitFunction a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exit function"@en ;
    skos:definition "The function sys.exit() ends a program before its last instruction. Programs always terminate when execution reaches the bottom of the instructions, but a call to sys.exit() terminates them earlier; because the function lives in the sys module, the module has to be imported first (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ExitFunction> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExitFunction" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EarlyExit .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExitFunction .
pg:KeyboardInterruptStop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Interrupting with Control-C"@en ;
    skos:definition "Pressing Control-C stops a program that is running. The book says that if a bug has caused an infinite loop, the key combination sends a KeyboardInterrupt error to the program and causes it to stop immediately; the same effect can be reached with the Stop button of the Mu editor (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_KeyboardInterruptStop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-KeyboardInterruptStop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EarlyExit .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:KeyboardInterruptStop .
pg:ReturnFromLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Return from a loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "A return statement ends a function and hands a value back to the place that called it, and a loop inside the function ends together with it. Chapter 1 showed how to call functions; writing your own is, in the book's words, the topic of the next chapter, so this section gives only what is needed to understand the effect on loops (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ReturnFromLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReturnFromLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EarlyExit .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReturnFromLoop .
pg:Skipping a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Skipping"@en ;
    skos:definition "Skipping means giving up one iteration of a loop without giving up the loop. The continue statement is the tool: it ends the current pass through the block and starts the next one (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_Skipping> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Skipping" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopControl .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Skipping .
pg:ContinueStatement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Continue statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "A continue statement jumps back to the start of the loop and reevaluates the loop's condition. The book describes it as what happens when the program execution reaches a continue statement, and notes that this is also what happens when the execution reaches the end of the loop (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ContinueStatement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ContinueStatement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Skipping .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ContinueStatement .
pg:LoopCompletion a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Loop completion"@en ;
    skos:definition "Loop completion is about what is true after a loop has ended: whether it ended by exhausting its supply or by a break, what value the loop variable has, and which of several possible endings occurred. The else clause of a loop and the lasting value of the loop variable are the two features of Python that make this visible (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_LoopCompletion> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoopCompletion" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopControl .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoopCompletion .
pg:LoopElseClause a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Loop else clause"@en ;
    skos:definition "A loop can have an else clause, which runs when the loop finishes without executing break. In a for loop the clause is executed after the final iteration, and in a while loop after the condition becomes false; in either kind it is not executed if the loop was ended by a break (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_LoopElseClause> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoopElseClause" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopCompletion .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoopElseClause .
pg:LoopVariableScope a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Loop variable scope"@en ;
    skos:definition "The loop variable of a for statement is an ordinary variable, and it lives on after the loop. The reference documentation states that the names in the target list are not deleted when the loop is finished, but if the sequence is empty they will not have been assigned to at all by the loop (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_LoopVariableScope> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoopVariableScope" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopCompletion .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoopVariableScope .
pg:LoopEnding a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Endings of a loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "A loop can end in several ways, and it matters which one happened. The first is that its condition becomes false, in a while loop, or that its iterator is exhausted, in a for loop."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_LoopEnding> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoopEnding" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopCompletion .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoopEnding .
pg:ExceptionHandling a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exceptions and warnings"@en ;
    skos:definition "Exceptions and warnings are the signals through which Python reports that something has gone wrong or looks wrong. An exception interrupts the normal flow of the program; a warning reports a suspicious construct and lets the program go on."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ExceptionHandling> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExceptionHandling" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoopControl .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExceptionHandling .
pg:ExceptionSignal a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exception"@en ;
    skos:definition "An exception is an error that is detected while a program runs. The Python tutorial explains that even if a statement or expression is syntactically correct, it may cause an error when an attempt is made to execute it, and that errors detected during execution are called exceptions and are not unconditionally fatal (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ExceptionSignal> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExceptionSignal" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExceptionHandling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExceptionSignal .
pg:FinallyClause a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Finally clause"@en ;
    skos:definition "A finally clause is the part of a try statement that is intended to define clean-up actions that must be executed under all circumstances. The tutorial states that the clause executes as the last task before the try statement completes, and that it runs whether or not the try statement produces an exception (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_FinallyClause> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FinallyClause" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExceptionHandling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FinallyClause .
pg:SyntaxWarning a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Syntax warning"@en ;
    skos:definition "A SyntaxWarning is a message issued by the compiler for code that is legal but probably a mistake. Unlike a SyntaxError, it does not prevent the program from running."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_SyntaxWarning> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SyntaxWarning" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExceptionHandling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SyntaxWarning .
pg:Module a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Module"@en ;
    skos:definition "A module is a unit of Python code that a program can load and use, and this branch of the chapter explains how to do so. The book introduces the idea in one passage: Python comes with a set of modules called the standard library, and each module is a Python program that contains a related group of functions that can be embedded in your programs (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_Module> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Module" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Module .
pg:ImportForm a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Import form"@en ;
    skos:definition "An import form is one of the ways of writing an import statement. The chapter shows three: the plain import of one module, the import of several modules in one statement, and the star import; the from form, which names the wanted names, completes the picture (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ImportForm> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImportForm" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Module .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImportForm .
pg:ImportStatement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Import statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The import statement loads a module and makes it available to the program. The book says that before the functions of a module can be used, the module must be imported, and that an import statement consists of the import keyword, the name of the module and, optionally, more module names separated by commas (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ImportStatement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImportStatement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImportForm .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImportStatement .
pg:MultipleImport a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Multiple import"@en ;
    skos:definition "A single import statement can name several modules, separated by commas. The book's example imports four of them at once: import random, sys, os, math (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_MultipleImport> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MultipleImport" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImportForm .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MultipleImport .
pg:StarImport a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Star import"@en ;
    skos:definition "The star import brings all the public names of a module into the program, so that they can be used without the module's name in front. The book writes it as the from keyword, the module name, the import keyword and a star, as in from random import *, and says that calls to the functions in random then no longer need the random."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_StarImport> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StarImport" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImportForm .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StarImport .
pg:FromImport a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "From import"@en ;
    skos:definition "The from form of the import statement imports chosen names of a module directly into the program. After from math import sqrt, the function is called as sqrt(16), without the prefix math."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_FromImport> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FromImport" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImportForm .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FromImport .
pg:ModuleBasics a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Module basics"@en ;
    skos:definition "The module basics are the facts about modules that every import relies on: that a module is a file with its own namespace, that Python comes with a standard library of ready-made modules, that names inside a module are reached with a dotted name, that Python searches a list of places to find a module, and that a file with the wrong name can hide a real module."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ModuleBasics> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModuleBasics" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Module .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModuleBasics .
pg:ModuleFile a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Module file"@en ;
    skos:definition "A module is a file that contains Python definitions and statements. The tutorial states that the file name is the module name with the suffix .py appended, and that within a module the module's name is available as the value of the global variable __name__ (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ModuleFile> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModuleFile" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModuleBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModuleFile .
pg:StandardLibrary a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Standard library"@en ;
    skos:definition "The standard library is the set of modules that Python comes with. The book distinguishes it from the built-in functions: all Python programs can call a basic set of built-in functions, including print(), input() and len(), while the modules of the standard library each have to be imported (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_StandardLibrary> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StandardLibrary" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModuleBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StandardLibrary .
pg:ModuleNamespace a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Module namespace"@en ;
    skos:definition "A namespace is the place where a variable is stored, and every module has its own. The glossary says that namespaces support modularity by preventing naming conflicts, and that they aid readability by making it clear which module implements a function: writing random.seed() or itertools.islice() makes clear that those functions are implemented by the random and itertools modules (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ModuleNamespace> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModuleNamespace" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModuleBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModuleNamespace .
pg:ModuleSearchPath a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Module search path"@en ;
    skos:definition "The module search path is the list of places in which Python looks for a module that has been imported. The tutorial explains that for a module named spam the interpreter first searches for a built-in module with that name, and if none is found it searches for a file named spam.py in the directories given by the variable sys.path (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ModuleSearchPath> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModuleSearchPath" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModuleBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModuleSearchPath .
pg:ModuleNameClash a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Overwriting a module name"@en ;
    skos:definition "A file that has the name of a module hides that module. The book gives the warning in a box of its own: when you save your Python scripts, take care not to give them a name that is used by one of Python's modules, such as random.py, sys.py, os.py or math.py (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ModuleNameClash> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModuleNameClash" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModuleBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModuleNameClash .
pg:StandardModule a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Standard modules in the chapter"@en ;
    skos:definition "The two standard modules of the chapter are random, which supplies the random numbers of the two games, and sys, which supplies sys.exit() and gives access to the interpreter. Both are imported with an ordinary import statement and used through dotted names (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_StandardModule> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StandardModule" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Module .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StandardModule .
pg:RandomModule a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Random module"@en ;
    skos:definition "The random module provides functions that generate pseudo-random numbers, and the chapter uses one of them, random.randint(). The library documentation defines it: random.randint(a, b) returns a random integer N such that a <= N <= b, and it is an alias for randrange(a, b+1) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_RandomModule> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RandomModule" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:StandardModule .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RandomModule .
pg:SysModule a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sys module"@en ;
    skos:definition "The sys module gives a program access to the interpreter that runs it. The tutorial singles it out as one particular module that deserves attention, and states that it is built into every Python interpreter (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_SysModule> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SysModule" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:StandardModule .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SysModule .
pg:ModernPractice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Modern practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "Modern practice is what current Python and its community add to the chapter of the book. The book teaches loops and imports as they are needed by a beginner; an advanced course also has to know the idioms that Python has gained since, the habits that the official style guide asks for, and the places where the language itself now warns about dangerous code (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ModernPractice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModernPractice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModernPractice .
pg:ExpressionStyle a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Expression style"@en ;
    skos:definition "Expression style is the way in which the same behaviour can be written more clearly. Two points belong to it in this chapter: the assignment expression, which lets a loop read a value and test it in a single line, and the import conventions of PEP 8, which fix where and how imports are written (Angelico et al., 2018) (Van Rossum et al., 2001)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ExpressionStyle> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExpressionStyle" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModernPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExpressionStyle .
pg:AssignmentExpressionLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Assignment expression loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "An assignment expression gives a name to a value inside an expression, and it is written NAME := expression. PEP 572 defines it: the value of the expression is the same as the value of the incorporated expression, with the additional side effect that the target is assigned that value; the operator became informally known as the walrus operator (Angelico et al., 2018)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_AssignmentExpressionLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AssignmentExpressionLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExpressionStyle .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AssignmentExpressionLoop .
pg:ImportStyle a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Import style"@en ;
    skos:definition "PEP 8, the style guide for Python code, has a section on imports. It says that imports should usually be on separate lines, that they are always put at the top of the file, just after any module comments and docstrings (the string at the start of a module or function that documents it) and before module globals and constants, and that wildcard imports should be avoided (Van Rossum et al., 2001)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_ImportStyle> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImportStyle" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExpressionStyle .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImportStyle .
pg:Hazard a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Hazard"@en ;
    skos:definition "A hazard is a construct that looks harmless but makes a program behave wrongly, often without any error message. Two loop-related hazards are documented in the official sources the chapter draws on: changing a collection while looping over it, and leaving a finally block with a control-flow statement (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_Hazard> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Hazard" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModernPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Hazard .
pg:MutationWhileIterating a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Mutation while iterating"@en ;
    skos:definition "Changing a collection while a loop is walking through it is tricky to get right. The tutorial says so in its section on the for statement: code that modifies a collection while iterating over that same collection can be tricky, and it is usually more straightforward to loop over a copy of the collection or to create a new collection (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_MutationWhileIterating> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MutationWhileIterating" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Hazard .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MutationWhileIterating .
pg:FinallyExit a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Finally exit"@en ;
    skos:definition "Leaving a finally block with a return, break or continue statement discards the exception that was on its way. The reference documentation states that if the finally clause executes one of these statements, the saved exception is discarded, and its example is a function that divides by zero in a try block and returns 42 from the finally clause, so that the function returns 42 and no exception is seen (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch03#S_FinallyExit> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FinallyExit" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Hazard .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FinallyExit .
